==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Onyx Egg: N207''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Tycoon Cat''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit#Behemoth Stones|Behemoth Stones]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
==='''Normal/Evolved Form'''===
These forms are <u>useless by design</u>. Similarly to the other [[:Category:Ancient Eggs|Ancient Eggs]], this unit is not meant to be used before its True Form. However, due to its somewhat low cost and quick recharge, it may be used as an emergency meatshield in [[Underground Labyrinth]] or [[Legend Quest]] if you have no use for its True Form.

==='''True Form'''===
A very decent crowd-controller. What you see is what you get with Tycoon Cat, being a stackable anti-Angel/Dark [[Weaken|Weakener]] with very low damage but high range and good pierce via [[Wave Attack#Mini-Wave|Mini-Wave]]. He outranges a majority of basic peons by a wide margin and can exploit melee pushers like [[Boraphim]] and [[Razorback]] to proc status effects on backliners like [[Cala Maria]].

Angels and Darks are notorious for possessing extravagantly high DPS that utterly shreds through any defensive wall. Tycoon Cat can greatly aid your respective meatshields like [[Rocker Cat (Rare Cat)|Ramen Cat]] and [[Jetpack Cat (Rare Cat)|Jetpack Cat]] in surviving far longer than they have any business to against these monsters. They're not as squishy as they appear to be either, since they can survive a hit from a majority of Weakened enemies, repositioning with one of their 2 knockbacks. This isn't even mentioning how cheap they are!

Tycoon Cat's [[Behemoth Slayer]] is utterly useless. Offensively, ~1460 DPS is nothing in the face of most Behemoths' inflated stats. Defensively, Tycoon Cat has 18,820 EHP, which barely provides any meaningful survivability that doesn't fall flat in practice. To make matters worse, all relevant Behemoths either innately outrange Tycoon Cat, or pierce into him, with the sole exception of [[Ragin' Gory]], who you'd be better off killing outright. You can't monopolize good stats, as it turns out.

